The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
rc = request_irq(ms->irq0, mpc52xx_spi_irq, 0, "mpc5200-spi-modf", ms);
rc |= request_irq(ms->irq1, mpc52xx_spi_irq, 0, "mpc5200-spi-spif", ms);
INIT_WORK(&ms->work, mpc52xx_spi_wq);
```
Reason:
These statements allocate IRQ handlers and initialize a work queue. If `spi_register_controller()` subsequently fails, the error path (`err_register`) originally did not free these IRQs or cancel the work. This leaves the IRQs and work active after the driver resources (e.g., the `ms` structure) are released, leading to a use-after-free when an interrupt fires or the work runs.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
free_irq(ms->irq0, ms);
free_irq(ms->irq1, ms);
cancel_work_sync(&ms->work);
```
Reason:
These statements are added to the `err_register` error path, right before the existing cleanup for GPIOs and other resources. They ensure that if controller registration fails, the previously registered IRQs are freed and the scheduled work is cancelled synchronously, preventing any further use of the freed driver data.

